        "description": {
          "history": "Despite its name, the Marinara contains no seafood whatsoever. It is actually the oldest documented pizza still in existence, first recorded in 1734. The name comes from its association with marinai (sailors) — specifically the wives of fishermen who prepared this simple, non-perishable pizza for their husbands returning from fishing trips in the Bay of Naples. It is also believed that sea captains provisioned their ships with tomatoes, garlic, and oil — the very ingredients of this pizza — because these ingredients kept well during long voyages. The Marinara predates the Margherita by over 150 years and is considered the ancestral pizza from which all others descend.",
          "ingredients": "San Marzano crushed tomatoes, raw garlic (sliced thin), dried Sicilian oregano, extra-virgin olive oil, and optionally a few Gaeta olives or anchovies. It is entirely vegan in its classic form — no cheese, no meat.",
          "preparation": "Dough is stretched and topped with crushed San Marzano tomatoes, scattered with thin garlic slices, generously seasoned with dried oregano, and finished with a circular drizzle of olive oil. It bakes in the wood-fired oven at high temperature, emerging with a fragrant, deeply flavored tomato base and crisp garlic edges. No cheese is added at any stage.",
          "culture": "The Marinara is the purist's pizza — humble, intense, and completely honest. In Naples it is regarded as the senior pizza, the one that reveals the most about a pizzeria's quality of ingredients and oven mastery. It is also fully vegan, making it one of the oldest plant-based fast foods in Western culinary history.",
          "variations": "Some modern pizzaioli add capers, anchovies, or a scattering of Taggiasca olives to the Marinara, giving it additional brininess. A variant called Pizza all'Aglio e Olio replaces tomato with garlic-infused oil for a white version of similar simplicity."
        }
