          "history": "The name Diavola — 'Devil's Pizza' — refers to its fiery heat, earned through the generous use of spicy Calabrian salami (salame piccante) and chili oil. It originated in southern Italy, particularly in Calabria and Campania, regions with a tradition of bold, spicy cooking. The pizza became popular throughout Italy in the mid-20th century as pizzerias sought to offer a more assertive, adult flavor profile alongside the classic Margherita. Today it is one of the most popular pizzas in Italy and across the world.",
          "ingredients": "Tomato sauce, fior di latte mozzarella, spicy Calabrian salame piccante (thinly sliced), Calabrian chili oil or fresh red chili, and dried oregano. Some versions add a final drizzle of chili-infused honey for a sweet-heat contrast.",
