    "id": 52,
    "name": "Lasagna",
    "cuisine": "Italian",
    "origin": "Bologna, Emilia-Romagna, Italy",
    "year": "14th Century",
    "type": "Non-Veg",
    "images": [
      "italian/lasagna1.jpeg",
      "italian/lasagna2.jpeg",
      "italian/lasagna3.jpeg",
      "italian/lasagna4.jpeg",
      "italian/lasagna5.jpeg"
    ],
    "description": {
      "history": "Lasagna holds the distinction of being one of the oldest pasta dishes in the Western world. The Latin word lagana — referring to thin sheets of pasta — appears in texts from ancient Rome, specifically in the writings of the poets Horace and Cicero, suggesting that flat pasta sheets have been eaten in Italy for over two thousand years. The medieval lasagna, recorded in 14th-century manuscripts from Naples (the Liber de Coquina, one of the oldest Italian cookbooks) and England, was a dish of layered pasta sheets boiled and served with cheese and spices — no tomato, no béchamel, no meat ragù, since all these ingredients came later. The modern lasagna as we know it — layered pasta, slow-cooked meat ragù, béchamel sauce, and Parmigiano-Reggiano — was codified in the culinary tradition of Bologna and Emilia-Romagna by at least the 18th century. The green pasta sheets of Lasagna Verdi alla Bolognese (made with spinach) are unique to the Bologna tradition and among the most visually striking presentations in Italian cooking. Lasagna became internationally popular through the waves of Italian emigration in the late 19th and 20th centuries, with Italian-American lasagna becoming a cultural institution in its own right.",
      "ingredients": "Fresh pasta sheets (egg pasta, sometimes mixed with spinach for the green Bolognese version), ragù alla bolognese (slow-cooked beef and pork with soffritto, wine, tomato, and milk), béchamel sauce (butter, flour, whole milk, nutmeg), and generous amounts of Parmigiano-Reggiano. Some Neapolitan versions incorporate ricotta, mozzarella, hard-boiled eggs, and Neapolitan salami — a much richer and more baroque construction than the austere Bolognese version.",
      "preparation": "The preparation of authentic lasagna alla bolognese is a multi-stage, multi-hour undertaking. The ragù is made first and cooked for a minimum of 3 hours. The béchamel is made separately — a classic white sauce thickened with butter and flour and flavored with nutmeg. Fresh pasta sheets are boiled briefly, shocked in ice water, and patted dry. The lasagna is assembled in a deep baking dish: a thin layer of ragù, then pasta, then béchamel, then ragù, then Parmigiano — the layers repeated 6–8 times. The final layer is pasta topped with béchamel, ragù, and a generous snowfall of Parmigiano. The dish is baked at 180°C for 30–40 minutes until golden and bubbling, then rested for 15–20 minutes before cutting — resting is critical for the layers to set and hold their shape when served.",
      "culture": "In Italy, lasagna is the quintessential Sunday and celebration dish — the food made for weddings, Easter Sunday lunches, Christmas gatherings, and the return of family members from abroad. Making lasagna from scratch — rolling the pasta by hand, cooking the ragù for hours — is an act of love and a statement of domestic prowess. In Bologna, lasagna skill is practically a prerequisite for social standing. The dish has become internationally synonymous with Italian-American cooking and has been immortalized in popular culture as one of humanity's great comfort foods.",
      "variations": "Lasagna al Forno (oven-baked with béchamel and ragù) is the Bolognese canon. Lasagna alla Napoletana uses ricotta, mozzarella, hard-boiled eggs, and local salami between layers. Lasagna Bianca (white lasagna) omits tomato entirely, using only béchamel, mushrooms, and cheese. Vegetarian lasagna replaces ragù with roasted vegetables (zucchini, eggplant, peppers) and ricotta."
    },
    "variations": []
