        "description": {
          "history": "Sichuan Red Oil Wonton (红油抄手, hóng yóu chāoshǒu) is one of Chengdu's most beloved street foods — a completely different expression of wonton compared to the Cantonese soup version, dressed in chili oil and sesame-vinegar sauce rather than served in broth. The name 'chaoshou' (抄手, literally 'crossed hands') refers to the folding technique used for this regional wonton style, which resembles the gesture of crossing one's hands in front of the body. The dish developed within Chengdu's rich noodle and street food culture and has been sold by Chengdu vendors for well over a century.",
          "ingredients": "Thin wheat wrappers, pork filling seasoned with ginger, soy sauce, and Sichuan seasonings. The dressing: homemade chili oil (made from dried chili flakes bloomed in hot oil with Sichuan peppercorns, star anise, and other spices), soy sauce, Chinkiang vinegar, sesame paste, sugar, garlic, and generous Sichuan peppercorn powder.",
          "preparation": "Wontons are boiled until cooked through and drained. They are placed in the serving bowl and dressed with the chili oil mixture — mixed tableside or pre-mixed in the kitchen. The red chili oil pools among the wontons and coats the wrappers, creating the characteristic vivid red appearance. Spring onion and additional peppercorn powder finish the dish.",
          "culture": "Red oil wonton is a Chengdu breakfast and snack staple — sold in small street-side shops that open at 6am and sell out by mid-morning. The combination of silky wonton wrappers and the numbing-spicy chili oil sauce is one of the most direct experiences of the mala philosophy available in Sichuan street food. Many Chengdu people eat red oil wonton several times per week.",
          "variations": "A sweet and sour version replaces some of the chili oil with additional vinegar for a tangier profile. A sesame-forward version increases the sesame paste proportion for a nuttier, milder result. Some shops add a spoonful of pork broth to the bottom of the bowl before adding the wontons and sauce."
        }
