        "id": 2101,
        "name": "Salmorejo",
        "cuisine": "Spanish",
        "origin": "Córdoba, Andalusia, Spain",
        "year": "19th Century",
        "type": "Veg",
        "description": "Salmorejo is Córdoba's definitive answer to gazpacho — a thicker, richer, creamier cold tomato preparation that uses fewer ingredients but achieves an even more luxurious texture and a more concentrated, intense tomato flavor than its better-known Andalusian cousin. While gazpacho incorporates multiple vegetables to create a complex, layered flavor profile, salmorejo is made from only four core ingredients: perfectly ripe tomatoes, day-old white bread, garlic, and extra virgin olive oil from Córdoba's own extraordinary olive groves, blended together for a long time until silky smooth and emulsified to a consistency closer to a thick purée than a soup. The ratio of bread to tomato is much higher in salmorejo than in gazpacho, which is what produces the characteristic thick body — the bread acts as both thickener and emulsifier, absorbing the tomato juice and olive oil into a homogeneous, spoonable mass. The result is a brilliant, tomato-red preparation with an almost pudding-like viscosity that coats the back of a spoon and has a velvety, rich mouthfeel from the thoroughly emulsified olive oil and bread. There is no vinegar in traditional salmorejo, which distinguishes it sharply from gazpacho — the acidity comes entirely from the natural tartness of the tomatoes, resulting in a purer, cleaner, more concentrated tomato flavor. It is served thoroughly chilled, topped with thin strips of Serrano ham cured in the mountains of Huelva and crumbled hard-boiled egg — the salt of the ham, the richness of the yolk, and the cold, creamy purée create a combination of extraordinary balance. Salmorejo reflects Córdoba's intense summer climate and the Andalusian genius for creating deeply satisfying, nourishing foods from the most minimal set of ingredients.",
