        "id": 6202,
        "name": "Cocido Montañés",
        "cuisine": "Spanish",
        "origin": "Cantabria, Spain",
        "year": "17th Century",
        "type": "Non-Veg",
        "description": "Cocido Montañés — Mountain Stew — is the distinctive Cantabrian cousin of Cocido Madrileño, a robust and deeply warming pot stew from the green, rainy mountains of northern Spain that makes the crucial substitution of white beans (alubias) for the chickpeas of the central Spanish original, reflecting the different agricultural traditions of the humid north Atlantic coast where white beans, not chickpeas, are the traditional legume of the region. The substitution of white beans for chickpeas is not merely an ingredient swap — it fundamentally changes the character of the dish. White beans cook faster, have a creamier, more delicate flavor, and break down more easily into the broth, creating a stew with a thicker, more unified consistency than the cleaner-broth Cocido Madrileño. The meats used in Cocido Montañés reflect the pig-farming traditions of Cantabria: fresh pork ribs, salted pork belly, morcilla from the mountains — which differs from the Madrid version in its higher proportion of rice and more subtle spicing — and chorizo made from local mountain pigs. The distinguishing vegetable element is collard greens (berza), the dark, sturdy leafy green that grows abundantly in Cantabria's cool, wet climate and that provides both nutritional density and a slightly bitter counterpoint to the richness of the meats and the creaminess of the beans. Paprika is used generously, turning the broth a deep, warm orange-red. Cocido Montañés is eaten as a single-course meal rather than the three-course service of Cocido Madrileño — everything is served together in a deep bowl or from a communal pot, which reflects the less formal, more rustic mountain origins of the dish.",
