        "description": {
          "history": "Mole Verde represents an entirely different philosophical branch of the mole family from the dark, chocolate-enriched preparations most internationally associated with Mexican mole. It derives from the ancient Mesoamerican tradition of fresh herb and seed sauces that predates Spanish colonization — pumpkin seeds (pepitas) were a staple of Aztec cooking, and fresh herbs including epazote and tomatillos were used in sauces long before the dried chili-based moles evolved. Mole Verde is thus, in some senses, the most historically ancient of the moles: closest to what pre-Columbian Mexican sauces may have tasted like before the integration of European ingredients.",
          "ingredients": "Tomatillos (fresh or lightly roasted). Fresh serrano or jalapeño chilis. Pumpkin seeds (pepitas — toasted in a dry pan until they begin to pop and turn golden, then ground into a paste that forms the sauce's body). Fresh epazote (the distinctive pungent herb without which an authentic Mole Verde loses its Oaxacan identity). Fresh cilantro. Hoja santa (anise-flavored herb). Garlic and white onion. Chicken or vegetable stock. Lard or oil.",
          "preparation": "Unlike the elaborate multi-day process of dark moles, Mole Verde can be prepared in a few hours — its character comes from freshness rather than long cooking and deep reduction. Pumpkin seeds are toasted and ground. Tomatillos, chilis, garlic, and onion are blended raw or with minimal cooking. All green ingredients are combined and blended until smooth, then fried in hot lard — the contact with the hot fat transforms the raw, sharp green flavors into something rounder and more complex. Stock is added and the mole simmers briefly, preserving the vivid green color and fresh flavor.",
          "culture": "Mole Verde is the most approachable and everyday-appropriate of Oaxaca's seven moles. Its freshness and relatively lighter flavor profile make it suitable for simpler occasions — family lunches, weekday dinners — where the elaborate dark moles would be excessive. It also represents the most accessible entry point into the mole tradition for those unfamiliar with the category, its tomatillo tang and herbal freshness being more immediately legible than the complex bitterness of Mole Negro.",
          "variations": "Pepián is the closest relative — a pure seed-based sauce that may be considered a simplified form of Mole Verde. A version using only pumpkin seeds and no tomatillo (pepián seco) is made in various parts of central Mexico. Mole Verde Guerrerense (from Guerrero state) adds hierba santa more aggressively and uses a different ratio of seeds to create a distinctly regional character."
        }
