        "description": {
          "history": "Enchiladas Verdes — built on the bright, tart tomatillo-based green sauce that is one of Mexican cooking's most distinctive contributions to world gastronomy — represent the fresh, herb-forward branch of the enchilada family. Tomatillos, the small husk-covered fruit related to but distinct from the tomato, were cultivated in Mesoamerica for thousands of years before the Spanish arrived, and the green sauce tradition that combines them with fresh herbs and chilis is one of the most ancient in Mexican cooking. Enchiladas Verdes as a specifically defined preparation crystallized in the colonial period and became particularly associated with Mexico City's restaurant culture.",
          "ingredients": "Fresh tomatillos (roasted or boiled until soft). Fresh serrano chilis (2–4 depending on heat preference). Garlic, white onion. Fresh cilantro. Sometimes a small amount of Mexican crema to mellow the tomatillo's tartness. Corn tortillas. Poached shredded chicken cooked directly in the salsa verde. Queso fresco, Mexican crema, sliced white onion, fresh cilantro for garnish.",
          "preparation": "Tomatillos and chilis roasted under the broiler until charred and soft (10–12 minutes). Blended with garlic, onion, cilantro, and a ladle of chicken stock. Fried in hot oil until the color darkens slightly and the raw flavor cooks out. Chicken added and simmered in the sauce for 15 minutes — this crucial step melds the chicken and sauce into a unified flavor. Tortillas dipped in the warm green sauce, filled with the sauced chicken, rolled. More sauce over the top. Finished with crema, queso fresco, onion.",
          "culture": "Enchiladas Verdes are among the most ordered dishes at Mexico City's comida corrida restaurants, particularly popular at lunchtime for their relative lightness compared to the richness of red or mole enchiladas. The bright green color and fresh tomatillo flavor make them the most visually and palatally refreshing of the enchilada family. In Guerrero state, Enchiladas Verdes con salsa de pepita (pumpkin seed-enriched green sauce) is a regional specialty.",
          "variations": "Enchiladas with green mole (Mole Verde) in Oaxaca. A version adding avocado to the sauce for creaminess. Mexico City-style with Mexican crema blended into the sauce. Vegetarian versions using roasted zucchini, squash blossom, or mushroom filling."
        }
