        "images": [
          "mexican/enchiladas-suizas1.jpeg",
          "mexican/enchiladas-suizas2.jpeg",
          "mexican/enchiladas-suizas3.jpeg",
          "mexican/enchiladas-suizas4.jpeg",
          "mexican/enchiladas-suizas5.jpeg"
        ],
        "description": {
          "history": "Enchiladas Suizas — 'Swiss Enchiladas' — are a Mexico City creation whose unusual name derives from their extravagant use of cream and cheese, characteristics that mid-20th century Mexican diners associated with Swiss dairy abundance. They were created and popularized at Sanborns, Mexico City's iconic café chain (founded 1903 by American brothers Walter and Frank Sanborn), specifically at the flagship Casa de los Azulejos location in the historic center, and quickly became a beloved staple of middle-class Mexico City restaurant dining in the 1950s and 1960s. Sanborns' Enchiladas Suizas — rich with cream, bubbling with melted Oaxacan cheese, and perfumed with roasted tomatillo — became a signature preparation so beloved that it has defined Mexican cream-enriched enchiladas for seven decades.",
          "ingredients": "Tomatillos (roasted until charred and soft). Fresh jalapeño or serrano chilis. Garlic, white onion. Heavy Mexican crema (30–35% fat) — incorporated into the blended sauce to create a creamy, slightly tangy, ivory-tinged green sauce. Corn tortillas. Shredded poached chicken. Oaxacan quesillo (string cheese) or Monterey Jack, shredded generously for the top.",
          "preparation": "The tomatillo salsa is prepared as for Enchiladas Verdes, but crema is blended directly into the finished sauce, producing a lighter green color and significantly richer, smoother texture. The cream mellows the tomatillo's tartness and creates a silky, coating consistency. Tortillas are dipped, filled with chicken, rolled, placed in baking dish, covered with the cream-enriched sauce and a generous layer of cheese. Baked at 200°C until the cheese is golden, bubbling, and beginning to brown at the edges.",
          "culture": "Enchiladas Suizas represent Mexico City's mid-century embrace of European dairy richness within the Mexican enchilada framework — a dish that bridges indigenous tortilla and chili tradition with the cream-and-cheese aesthetic of European-influenced Mexican middle-class cooking. At Sanborns Casa de los Azulejos — one of Mexico City's most beautiful colonial buildings, decorated in Talavera tile — eating Enchiladas Suizas is a specific cultural experience connected to Mexico City's modern identity and its cosmopolitan aspirations of the 20th century.",
          "variations": "Versions using a combination of tomatillo and fresh cream cheese for an even richer sauce. A Monterrey variation uses the northern Mexican white cheese (queso blanco). A contemporary health-conscious version uses crema light and reduced-fat cheese."
        }
