        "type": "Veg",
        "images": [
          "mexican/guacamole-molcajete1.jpeg",
          "mexican/guacamole-molcajete2.jpeg",
          "mexican/guacamole-molcajete3.jpeg",
          "mexican/guacamole-molcajete4.jpeg",
          "mexican/guacamole-molcajete5.jpeg"
        ],
        "description": {
          "history": "The tradition of preparing guacamole tableside in a volcanic stone molcajete is both an ancient practice and a distinctly modern restaurant theater. The molcajete itself predates the Aztec empire — volcanic basalt mortars used for grinding chilis, cacao, seeds, and other ingredients have been found in archaeological sites throughout Mesoamerica dating back 6,000 years or more. The tableside preparation format as a restaurant service ritual developed in the 20th century as Mexican fine dining establishments sought ways to create memorable, interactive dining experiences while simultaneously demonstrating the quality and freshness of their guacamole preparation.",
          "ingredients": "A perfect tableside guacamole requires: a properly seasoned molcajete (not freshly made — a good molcajete requires months of breaking in, grinding dried corn and chilis repeatedly until the basalt pores are sealed with starch residue), ripe Hass avocados halved and carried to the table in their skins (preserving freshness until the last moment), fresh serrano chili, white onion, cilantro, lime, and coarse salt — all brought to the table in small individual bowls.",
          "preparation": "The server grinds chili with salt in the molcajete tableside, adding onion and cilantro. The avocado flesh is scooped in and mashed to the guest's specified texture (chunky or smooth). Lime juice is squeezed in, salt adjusted. Tomato added if desired. Served immediately in the molcajete itself, which is porous and continues to impart subtle flavor from its seasoned surface.",
          "culture": "The tableside molcajete guacamole is the most theatrical element of Mexican restaurant service and one of the most immediately engaging dining experiences Mexican cuisine offers to international visitors. It simultaneously demonstrates craftsmanship (the manual preparation skill of the server), freshness (preparation from raw ingredients in front of the diner), and cultural depth (the ancient molcajete and its pre-Columbian origins). Many Mexican fine dining restaurants consider this service element a signature of their identity.",
          "variations": "Some restaurants allow full customization of heat level and ingredient proportions. Upscale versions add truffle oil, crème fraîche, or rare chilis to the tableside preparation as premium options. A deconstructed version presents the components separately for individual assembly."
        }
