        "description": {
          "history": "Quiche au Chèvre et Tomates — Goat Cheese and Tomato Quiche — represents the meeting of France's greatest fresh cheese tradition (the Loire Valley's extraordinary chèvre production, which gives the world Sancerre, Crottin de Chavignol, and countless other goat cheeses) with the summer tomato abundance of Provence. It emerged in the late 20th century as French regional cooking became more celebrated and as goat cheese, once largely a local product, achieved national and international recognition.",
          "ingredients": "Blind-baked pâte brisée shell. Fresh chèvre (goat cheese — log-style Sainte-Maure de Touraine or Crottin de Chavignol, sliced or crumbled). Ripe tomatoes (halved cherry tomatoes or thin-sliced plum tomatoes, drained briefly to remove excess moisture). Fresh thyme or basil. Eggs, heavy cream, salt, white pepper. Black or green olives optional.",
          "preparation": "Crumbled or sliced goat cheese and tomatoes are arranged in the blind-baked shell. The custard (eggs and cream, lightly seasoned) is poured over. Fresh thyme leaves scattered on top. Baked at 160°C for 30 minutes until set. Served warm with a peppery green salad (rocket/arugula being the ideal complement to the goat cheese's acidity).",
          "culture": "This quiche has become a staple of French summer outdoor dining — picnic lunches in Provence, vineyard visits in the Loire, farmers' market lunch counters throughout the south of France. It is one of the preparations most associated with France's artisan food culture and with the revival of interest in regional chèvre production.",
          "variations": "A version adding Niçoise olives and anchovy for a more assertive Provençal flavor profile. A version using sun-dried tomatoes (tomates confites) rather than fresh for a more concentrated, year-round preparation. A version finishing the baked quiche with a scattering of fresh basil leaves and a drizzle of basil oil."
        }
