        "description": {
          "history": "Som Tum Poo — Green Papaya Salad with Crab — is the most intensely flavored and most adventurous version of the Thai papaya salad tradition, incorporating salted black crabs (pu dong) that are one of the most distinctive and culturally specific ingredients in the Isan culinary canon. These crabs — small freshwater paddy crabs caught in the rice fields of northeastern Thailand — are packed in salt and fermented for weeks until they develop an extraordinarily briny, concentrated, slightly funky character that adds oceanic depth to everything they touch.",
          "ingredients": "All ingredients of Som Tum Isan, with the addition of whole salted black crabs (pu dong) — small freshwater crabs preserved in salt, added whole to the mortar and broken apart during the pounding and tossing process. Pla ra is used alongside or instead of fish sauce. The crab shells and legs are pounded open to release their fermented interior juices directly into the salad.",
          "preparation": "The crabs are added to the mortar whole or halved and broken open with the pestle during the normal tossing process. Their fermented juices pour into the salad, coloring the dressing and intensifying its oceanic character. Diners pick the crab pieces from the salad and extract the soft interior from the shells at the table.",
          "culture": "Som Tum Poo is the version that most clearly distinguishes Isan food culture from central Thai cooking — it represents the fermented, funky, intensely savory flavor philosophy of the northeast, which embraces preserved and fermented ingredients in a way that central Thai cooking does not. Eating Som Tum Poo with confidence is a mark of respect for Isan food culture and an indication of genuine Thai food knowledge.",
          "variations": "A version using freshwater shrimp alongside the crab. A version using salted egg (khai khem) added to the mortar for additional richness. A Bangkok restaurant version uses cleaned crab claws rather than whole fermented crabs for a more approachable presentation."
        }
