          "ingredients": "Chicken. Marinade: fresh lemongrass, fresh cilantro roots, garlic, fish sauce, soy sauce, oyster sauce, palm sugar, turmeric. Additionally: dried galangal powder, dried long pepper (a northern Thai spice), makhwaen berry (northern Thai dried spice with a citrusy-numbing quality related to Sichuan peppercorn). Coconut milk in some versions. Northern Thai jaew (slightly different from Isan jaew, sometimes adding dried chili powder from local northern Thai varieties).",
          "preparation": "Identical grilling technique to the Isan version but with the northern Thai spice additions in the marinade creating a more complex, warming, slightly numbing flavor profile. The makhwaen berry in particular gives Chiang Mai Gai Yang its distinctive quality that sets it apart from the Isan version.",
          "culture": "Gai Yang Chiang Mai represents the Lanna culinary tradition's distinctly different approach to Thai cooking — the use of dried spices, wild herbs, and fermented ingredients that reflects the northern Thai highland culture and its ancient connection to trade routes through Burma and southern China. The best Gai Yang in Chiang Mai is served at roadside stalls on the roads leading out of the city, particularly famous on the Mae Ping river road.",
