        "type": "Veg",
        "images": [
          "russian/gurievskaya1.jpeg",
          "russian/gurievskaya2.jpeg",
          "russian/gurievskaya3.jpeg",
          "russian/gurievskaya4.jpeg",
          "russian/gurievskaya5.jpeg"
        ],
        "description": {
          "history": "Gurievskaya kasha (Гурьевская каша) is the most famous and most luxurious dessert kasha of Russian cuisine — a layered, baked semolina pudding with caramelized milk skins, nuts, and fruit preserves that is widely held to be one of the finest creations of 19th-century Russian aristocratic cooking. The dish is traditionally attributed to Count Dmitry Guriev, finance minister of Tsar Alexander I, who reportedly encountered the kasha at the home of a retired major in 1814 and was so taken with it that he bought the major's serf cook in order to bring the recipe to St. Petersburg. The dish became a fixture of Imperial court cuisine and aristocratic dinner parties throughout the 19th century, and survived into the Soviet period as a celebratory dish in upscale restaurants.",
          "ingredients": "Semolina (manka). Whole milk and heavy cream (in roughly equal proportions — the high cream content is essential to producing the rich milk skins that define the dish). Sugar. Eggs. Vanilla. Salt. Butter. Walnuts and/or hazelnuts (lightly toasted and chopped). Almond meal (optional, traditional). Fruit preserves — typically apricot, sour cherry, or wild strawberry — and candied or glacé fruits. Sometimes a touch of cognac or Madeira for the adult version.",
          "preparation": "The signature element is the production of 'penki' (caramelized milk skins): cream is heated in a wide shallow pan and the resulting milk skin is repeatedly skimmed off with a fork as it forms, set aside, and the cream is reheated to form another skin — this is repeated 8–12 times, producing a stack of golden milk skins that are the heart of the dish. Meanwhile, semolina is cooked in milk with sugar, vanilla, and butter to a creamy porridge, then enriched with egg yolks (and sometimes whipped whites). The dish is assembled in layers in a buttered baking dish: a layer of semolina, a layer of milk skin, a sprinkling of nuts and a spoonful of fruit preserves, repeating the layers until the dish is full. The top is sprinkled with sugar and the whole thing is baked at moderate heat until golden, sometimes finished briefly under the broiler for a caramelized top. Served warm with additional fruit preserves spooned alongside.",
          "culture": "Gurievskaya kasha is the showpiece dessert of pre-revolutionary Russian cuisine, a dish whose multi-hour preparation marks it as a celebration food rather than an everyday treat. It was reportedly the favorite dessert of Tsar Alexander III. In contemporary Russia, the dish has been revived by restaurants emphasizing Imperial-era cuisine, and it makes appearances at state banquets and gala dinners as a representative of Russia's haute culinary heritage.",
          "variations": "A version using rice instead of semolina (a less common traditional alternative). A simplified version where the milk skins are produced fewer times for home cooking. A version with brandied cherries layered in for an alcoholic adult version."
        }
