        "description": {
          "history": "Walnut tzatziki is a regional Greek variant from the mountainous interior of mainland Greece — particularly Epirus, western Macedonia, and parts of Thessaly — where walnut trees grow abundantly and walnuts are integrated into many traditional dishes. The addition of finely chopped walnuts gives the tzatziki additional richness, textural complexity, and a slightly sweeter, earthier flavor. The dish is closely related to Bulgarian and Albanian walnut-cucumber-yogurt dishes (such as Bulgarian tarator soup), reflecting the shared culinary geography of the southern Balkans.",
          "ingredients": "Strained Greek yogurt. Grated and drained cucumber. Garlic. Finely chopped walnuts (a generous handful — both mixed in and sprinkled on top). Extra-virgin olive oil (often walnut oil is used alongside or instead of olive oil for emphasis). Fresh dill or mint. Red wine vinegar. Salt and pepper.",
          "preparation": "Standard tzatziki preparation — cucumber drained, all ingredients mixed — with the addition of finely chopped walnuts folded in. The dish is rested for an hour and finished with extra walnuts and a drizzle of olive oil on top. The walnuts soften slightly as the tzatziki rests, but retain enough texture to give a pleasing contrast to the smooth yogurt.",
          "culture": "Walnut tzatziki appears on meze tables in Epirus and northern Greek tavernas, where the regional pride in mountain ingredients (walnuts, wild herbs, sheep's milk dairy) shapes the cuisine. The dish pairs particularly well with grilled lamb, kontosouvli, and Epirote pies. It also represents a bridge to broader Balkan walnut-yogurt traditions and demonstrates that Greek cuisine, while sharing a strong national identity, varies meaningfully by region.",
          "variations": "A version using almonds in place of or alongside walnuts. A version with the addition of pomegranate molasses for sweet-sour notes. A modern restaurant version with toasted walnuts and walnut oil."
        }
